sections.recommendation:
On aircraft missions with low altitude flight legs, the following actions are recommended:

1. Bird hazard analysis data for the area to be used should be consulted before flights: Local airports may have data available to pilots for migratory season assessments USAF Avian Hazard Avoidance System (AHAS) website ( http://www.usahas.com/ ) available for airfield and general risk review Nearby USAF airfields can tell pilot if they are in an elevated Bird Aircraft Strike Hazard (BASH) Phase II period (annual calendar periods of peak bird activity) Known migratory bird patterns may also indicate specific altitudes to avoid

2. The aircraft recognition lights should be turned on for flight below 10,000 feet;

3. The crew brief should include bird hazard threat management and Crew Resource Management (CRM) roles and responsibilities for visual look out;

4. During periods of high workload, when visual lookout might be reduced, consider climbing to reduce bird strike risk;

5. If a migratory season or a nearby USAF base is in BASH Phase II, avoid low-altitude flying, including takeoff/landing within +/- 1 hour from sunrise/sunset unless mission requirements dictate.

If a bird strike has occurred or is suspected:

1. Establish a climb away from the ground in order to safely assess aircraft systems, personnel injuries and controllability;

2. Land as soon as practical and inspect the aircraft prior to continuing flight; and

3. For windscreen strike, with two qualified pilots on board, consider visibility and injuries in determining who is the best pilot to land

sections.lesson_learned:
Once the program was put on a sound foundation for development through launch with the JWST rebaseline in 2011, NASA had to address the management issues identified by the independent review panel. The new JWST Program Director describes changes in five areas that have led, thus far, to considerably improved telescope development performance. The areas are:

- clearly defined authority, responsibility and accountability;

- improved communication;

- commitment to the established baseline;

- improved risk management;

- independent review and increased program planning and control capability and analysis.

sections.recommendation:
1. A project¿s risk classification and commensurate RM requirements should be highlighted and communicated throughout its lifecycle such that all its support organizations and stakeholders can have realistic expectations with regard to what to expect from the project¿s RM processes. This includes expecting an occasional loss as risk management does not mean risk elimination.

a. Centers should have a written RM procedure that details how the Agency requirements will be implemented. This procedure should be consulted whenever requests are made of the project.

2. Centers should establish a training program whereby the Agency and Center RM policies and procedures are taught to its employees. Particular attention should be given to;

a. how specific RM requirements are mapped to each type of project or mission and;

b. how the implementation of each requirement can be tailored to the fit within the project¿s scope.

An integrated, dedicated and experienced procurement/technical management team is essential beginning in the early phases of the Project. Advance procurement planning and effective technical management will ensure development of realistic schedules and will result in the most cost-effective acquisition strategies for the Project. A well-developed acquisition plan should also incorporate:

1. technology readiness and impact on the development cycle;

2. best approach(es) to meet the unique needs of the Project;

3. effective contract structure/contract type/incentive plans; and

4. work-arounds and dual procurement strategies, where appropriate.
